

# WHAT A COMMIT MESSAGE HERE IS FOR
#
# The subject line says what changed and why it was wrong before, in a sentence somebody can
# read a year from now without the diff. Not "fix REG010": what REG010 was doing, and what it
# does instead.
#
# The body carries the reasoning the diff cannot. This repository's rule is that a check is
# added when the failure it prevents has already happened once, and the commit is where that
# once is written down -- which repository hit it, what the wrong answer looked like, and why
# the cheap repair was the wrong one. If a decision was taken, the alternatives that lost go
# here too: the next person to reopen the question starts from the argument instead of from
# scratch.
#
# NO TRAILERS. No `Co-Authored-By`, no `Signed-off-by`, no `Generated with`. The message ends
# at the last paragraph.
#
# Six commits carried a `Co-Authored-By: Claude ...` line before this file existed, and the
# consequence was not cosmetic: GitHub derives its contributor list from commit metadata, so
# an agent appeared as a contributor to the repository and the only way out was rewriting
# fifteen commits and force pushing over a published branch. The line costs nothing to leave
# out and cannot be removed cheaply once it is in.
#
# Turn this file on for the repository:
#
#     git config commit.template .gitmessage
#
# It is per clone -- git does not ship a template with the code -- so it has to be run once
# by whoever clones, and `PROCESSES.md` says so where the processes are written down.
